\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The framework in one view. \textbf  {a}, A pathway as a cascade of stochastic layers; the degeneracy group $G_i$ collapses each layer's parameter space onto an orbit, and only orbit invariants are reportable downstream. \textbf  {b}, Interface algebra: for the generic affine interface $y=kx+b$ with unidentifiable gain, difference ratios, signs, counts and event times survive unconditionally; amplitude is killed by the group unless the gain is pinned from outside; fold change survives only where the basal term vanishes. \textbf  {c}, The three-leg criterion: a statistic crosses an interface if and only if it survives algebraically, is symbolically reachable, and fits the temporal bandwidth. The information-loss identity decomposes input entropy into transmitted information plus per-layer losses, identifying where the largest single loss occurs. \textbf  {d}, The three reference classes: calibration-free statistics, structural constants maintained by physics, and paid references maintained by active feedback.}}{5}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:schematic}{{1}{5}{The framework in one view. \textbf {a}, A pathway as a cascade of stochastic layers; the degeneracy group $G_i$ collapses each layer's parameter space onto an orbit, and only orbit invariants are reportable downstream. \textbf {b}, Interface algebra: for the generic affine interface $y=kx+b$ with unidentifiable gain, difference ratios, signs, counts and event times survive unconditionally; amplitude is killed by the group unless the gain is pinned from outside; fold change survives only where the basal term vanishes. \textbf {c}, The three-leg criterion: a statistic crosses an interface if and only if it survives algebraically, is symbolically reachable, and fits the temporal bandwidth. The information-loss identity decomposes input entropy into transmitted information plus per-layer losses, identifying where the largest single loss occurs. \textbf {d}, The three reference classes: calibration-free statistics, structural constants maintained by physics, and paid references maintained by active feedback}{figure.caption.1}{}}
