\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The static sensor. \textbf  {a}, Steady-state curve family of Eq.~(\ref {eq:gk}); the Hill coefficient follows the closed form $n_H=1+1/(2\kappa )$. \textbf  {b}, Degeneracy-group invariance: four parameter sets on one orbit produce one curve (max $|\Delta u|=0$ for the pure $(K_m,S_T)$ and rate rescalings; $2.2\times 10^{-16}$ for the mixed rate--enzyme action). \textbf  {c}, Fisher eigenspectrum at $\kappa =0.05$: the shape tier is well conditioned while the absolute tier carries an exact zero eigenvalue along the group orbit. \textbf  {d}, The closed-form Hill slope against numerical logit slopes; in the deep zero-order region only a one-sided bound on $\kappa $ is reportable. \textbf  {e}, Steady-state mutual information between a log-normal input ensemble and the noisy output has an interior optimum in $\kappa $; the zero-order limit is information-poor for all input widths.}}{8}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:pdpc}{{2}{8}{The static sensor. \textbf {a}, Steady-state curve family of Eq.~(\ref {eq:gk}); the Hill coefficient follows the closed form $n_H=1+1/(2\kappa )$. \textbf {b}, Degeneracy-group invariance: four parameter sets on one orbit produce one curve (max $|\Delta u|=0$ for the pure $(K_m,S_T)$ and rate rescalings; $2.2\times 10^{-16}$ for the mixed rate--enzyme action). \textbf {c}, Fisher eigenspectrum at $\kappa =0.05$: the shape tier is well conditioned while the absolute tier carries an exact zero eigenvalue along the group orbit. \textbf {d}, The closed-form Hill slope against numerical logit slopes; in the deep zero-order region only a one-sided bound on $\kappa $ is reportable. \textbf {e}, Steady-state mutual information between a log-normal input ensemble and the noisy output has an interior optimum in $\kappa $; the zero-order limit is information-poor for all input widths}{figure.caption.4}{}}
