\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Extended analysis of the covalent cycle. \textbf  {a}, Goldbeter--Koshland curve family across $\kappa $. \textbf  {b}, The four group generators verified to machine precision, with a counterexample action moving the curve by $1.45\times 10^{-1}$. \textbf  {c}, Fisher condition numbers: the shape block stays at $\chi \approx 19$--$33$ while the absolute block retains a numerical-zero eigenvalue at every $\kappa $. \textbf  {d}, Asymmetric-Michaelis curves; inset, the closed form of Eq.~(\ref {eq:hill}) against the asymmetry ratio. \textbf  {e}, Confidence-width inflation along the saturation axis (Table~\ref {tab:onesided}); below $\kappa \lesssim 0.01$ only a one-sided upper bound is reportable. \textbf  {f}, The positive-feedback switch: beyond $g^*=0.443$ the response folds, the apparent Hill coefficient rises steeply with feedback gain, and every bistable branch remains group-invariant.}}{20}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:pdpcfull}{{3}{20}{Extended analysis of the covalent cycle. \textbf {a}, Goldbeter--Koshland curve family across $\kappa $. \textbf {b}, The four group generators verified to machine precision, with a counterexample action moving the curve by $1.45\times 10^{-1}$. \textbf {c}, Fisher condition numbers: the shape block stays at $\chi \approx 19$--$33$ while the absolute block retains a numerical-zero eigenvalue at every $\kappa $. \textbf {d}, Asymmetric-Michaelis curves; inset, the closed form of Eq.~(\ref {eq:hill}) against the asymmetry ratio. \textbf {e}, Confidence-width inflation along the saturation axis (Table~\ref {tab:onesided}); below $\kappa \lesssim 0.01$ only a one-sided upper bound is reportable. \textbf {f}, The positive-feedback switch: beyond $g^*=0.443$ the response folds, the apparent Hill coefficient rises steeply with feedback gain, and every bistable branch remains group-invariant}{figure.caption.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Four worked instances. \textbf  {a}, Morphogen readout: per-boundary scaling coefficients of the gap-gene boundaries at three bcd dosages (84 boundaries; error bars, 68\% CI). Mid-embryo scaling is near 1 and falls monotonically with dose, the signature of threshold-crossing readout; the per-embryo anchor analysis gives $\alpha =-0.18$, excluding exact scaling at $8.3\sigma $. \textbf  {b}, Wnt/$\beta $-catenin fold change versus LiCl dose (digitised from \cite  {Goentoro2009Wnt}): plateau within $\pm 5\%$ between 7 and 49 mM, breakdown bracketed between 48.6 and 58.1 mM. \textbf  {c}, The M3R-to-Ca$^{2+}$ cascade: across-cell CV of the per-cell mean response dominates within-cell repeatability at every dose; per-cell rank is preserved ($\rho =0.58$--$0.90$) while across-experiment EC$_{50}$ values scatter over a 32-fold range. \textbf  {d}, Positive control for class-III payment: yeast volume recovery after osmotic shock collapses from 0.644 to 0.070 when the Hog1 maintenance loop is inhibited.}}{21}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:instances}{{4}{21}{Four worked instances. \textbf {a}, Morphogen readout: per-boundary scaling coefficients of the gap-gene boundaries at three bcd dosages (84 boundaries; error bars, 68\% CI). Mid-embryo scaling is near 1 and falls monotonically with dose, the signature of threshold-crossing readout; the per-embryo anchor analysis gives $\alpha =-0.18$, excluding exact scaling at $8.3\sigma $. \textbf {b}, Wnt/$\beta $-catenin fold change versus LiCl dose (digitised from \cite {Goentoro2009Wnt}): plateau within $\pm 5\%$ between 7 and 49 mM, breakdown bracketed between 48.6 and 58.1 mM. \textbf {c}, The M3R-to-Ca$^{2+}$ cascade: across-cell CV of the per-cell mean response dominates within-cell repeatability at every dose; per-cell rank is preserved ($\rho =0.58$--$0.90$) while across-experiment EC$_{50}$ values scatter over a 32-fold range. \textbf {d}, Positive control for class-III payment: yeast volume recovery after osmotic shock collapses from 0.644 to 0.070 when the Hog1 maintenance loop is inhibited}{figure.caption.6}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces One pattern, one record. \textbf  {a}, The analysis discipline applied identically to every system: derive the degeneracy group, identify the invariant statistics, freeze the decision lines, score blind, and record failures twice; below, the group action collapses parameter space onto one curve. \textbf  {b}, The stratification across the four deeply analysed systems (rows; companion papers \cite  {CompanionP53,CompanionChemo,CompanionGPCR}): absolute-scale quantities are unidentifiable, internally inconsistent, or unreliable, while scale-free statistics are identifiable, exact, and information-bearing. \textbf  {c}, The frozen-clause record of the blind adjudications: signed distance to each frozen decision line, in units of the line; hits, intermediates, falsifications and dataset-level limitations are plotted with the same prominence.}}{22}{figure.caption.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ledger}{{5}{22}{One pattern, one record. \textbf {a}, The analysis discipline applied identically to every system: derive the degeneracy group, identify the invariant statistics, freeze the decision lines, score blind, and record failures twice; below, the group action collapses parameter space onto one curve. \textbf {b}, The stratification across the four deeply analysed systems (rows; companion papers \cite {CompanionP53,CompanionChemo,CompanionGPCR}): absolute-scale quantities are unidentifiable, internally inconsistent, or unreliable, while scale-free statistics are identifiable, exact, and information-bearing. \textbf {c}, The frozen-clause record of the blind adjudications: signed distance to each frozen decision line, in units of the line; hits, intermediates, falsifications and dataset-level limitations are plotted with the same prominence}{figure.caption.8}{}}
